          2011-07-15 15:48:17 UTC
        </span>

      </div>




<pre class="bz_comment_text">(In reply to <a href="show_bug.cgi?id=13240#c5">comment #5</a>)
<span class="quote">&gt; I think that changing it to &lt;data&gt; could make it too generic.</span >

As a publisher (front-end developer who builds sites for large companies) I would like to second Ian. Prior to HTML5, we were using hAtom to mark up articles and blog posts. When we were using hAtom, I heard frequent questions from other developers here regarding the way dates were marked up. The abbr, title and value-title patterns were confusing to developers that hadn't read the spec.

We have not had that problem with &lt;article&gt; + &lt;time&gt;. When training devs here on HTML5, folks immediately &quot;got&quot; what &lt;time&gt; was for. Also, having datetime be validate-able has meant it gets fixed if it's not properly formatted.</pre>
    </div>

